 ## Tests the lead must change (hand-built rows need the new mark)
 - tests/test_hardening_cloud.py::test_f4_startup_reads_capability_again and tests/test_hardening_cloud_b.py::test_f3_reaper_makes_stuck_sending_u_02, test_g8_earlier_unclear_send_is_never_r, test_g8_no_requeue_when_mode_unknown, test_g8_startup_waits_when_mode_unknown build a "killed mid-send" row by hand (`state='sending'`, `crm_done=1` or nothing). Such a row now needs `send_started=1` (and `crm_done=1`) to count as "may have sent"; without the mark it is a row that never sent and is put back to `pending` on any sender. The setup SQL is the only change.
 - tests/test_flow.py `EXPECT[("D1","blind")]` is now `(1, "sent", None)` (a kill before the send is put back and sent); changed in fix 7.
+
+- Round S1e (AI step after the claim): `run_followup` no longer asks the AI step before the claim. Before, the row was still `pending` while a slow model call ran, so the reaper (it drives `pending` rows older than the 2 s grace) started a second drive and the model was asked twice. Now the claim comes first and the AI step runs in `_after_claim`, so one claimed row means one model call. Contract line 21, rule (4), is replaced by: "Only the thread that won the claim asks the AI step, the CRM or the sender ... A failed AI step after the claim is one automatic try (`internal:<class>`, back to `pending`, `failed` at the cap)." Same effect as before for a failing AI step: one automatic try, row `pending`, `last_error` `internal:<class>`, `failed` at 5 tries; the difference is that `attempts` (the claim count) now also counts that try, as the entry above already says it does.
